Amblyopia
Opthalmology & ENT
Decreased best-corrected acuity, History of strabismus/refractive error, No structural ocular pathology

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-005
Reason for Visit: Sudden parental realization of profound unilateral visual deficit in a child following minor head trauma, fearing permanent neurological injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (child) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a presumed acute, severe unilateral vision loss discovered incidentally after a minor fall. Urgent medical assessment was strictly required to rule out acute traumatic intracranial hemorrhage, retinal detachment, or traumatic optic neuropathy. The severity of the reported visual deficit necessitated immediate evaluation prior to the next morning to secure neurological safety.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He bumped his head, and when I covered his good eye to check on him, he couldn't see anything. I'm terrified he's bleeding in his brain."
History of Present Illness: 6-year-old child had a minor ground-level fall 2 hours ago. During parental checking, they covered his left eye and discovered profound decreased visual acuity in the right eye. Parents report no loss of consciousness, vomiting, or altered behavior. Upon focused history taking, parents admit to a history of a 'lazy eye' (manifest strabismus turning inward) noticed occasionally when the child is tired, but never formally investigated.
Functional Status: Independent; playing normally prior to and after the fall.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme parental anxiety regarding acute traumatic brain injury.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 22 Kg
General: Child is alert, interactive, in zero distress, playing with a tablet.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity testing (Snellen equivalent) reveals a profound two-line discrepancy: 6/6 in the left eye, 6/24 in the right eye. No uncorrected relative afferent pupillary defect directly indicating intact anterior visual pathway transmission. Red reflex is symmetric and bright bilaterally. Ophthalmoscopy reveals no structural ocular pathology; optic discs sharp. Manifest strabismus visibly presenting as right esotropia. Fixation preference consistently favors the healthy left eye. Poor, uncoordinated tracking in the affected eye. Neurological exam: GCS 15, no focal motor/sensory deficits, cranial nerves intact. Head exam: minor superficial abrasion to right forehead, no bogginess or signs of basilar skull fracture.
Environmental Observations: Safe, child-friendly home.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Amblyopia (Strabismic) - Status: Stable (Incidentally discovered)
Differentials:

Traumatic Optic Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) and normal optic disc appearance.

Intracranial Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal GCS, absence of vomiting, intact neurological examination, and mechanism of injury being very low impact.

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal red reflex, normal fundoscopy, and lack of systemic visual complaints by the child prior to covering the good eye.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of a perceived acute profound unilateral visual loss following head trauma is a high-risk scenario necessitating immediate medical evaluation to exclude catastrophic neurological or intraocular injury. Comprehensive clinical and neurological examination confirmed the head trauma was trivial and entirely unrelated to the visual deficit. The objective findings of right-sided esotropia, unilateral poor acuity without structural pathology, and intact pupillary reflexes decisively confirmed pre-existing strabismic amblyopia. This urgent assessment prevented a traumatic, unnecessary pediatric emergency department presentation and radiation exposure (CT head) while facilitating definitive specialist follow-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Urgent referral to a Pediatric Ophthalmologist and Orthoptist for comprehensive assessment and commencement of occlusion therapy (patching).
Positive Behavioral Change: To prepare the child for future patching therapy, parents are instructed to introduce a 'pirate game' covering the good eye for 5 minutes daily with a reward immediately following. This operant conditioning replaces anxiety with a positive association, ensuring higher compliance when formal occlusion therapy is prescribed by the specialist.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to confirm ophthalmology appointment booking.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the child develops intractable vomiting, worsening headache, abnormal drowsiness, unequal pupils, or any focal weakness (post-concussion red flags).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of amblyopia is highly probable; structural pathology definitively ruled out clinically but warrants formal specialist imaging.
Clinical Course: The visual deficit is chronic. With aggressive specialist patching and visual rehabilitation before age 8, significant visual recovery is highly likely.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The head bump is minor, but monitor for concussion. If he becomes extremely sleepy, vomits repeatedly, or acts confused tonight, bring him to the Emergency Department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The brain is ignoring the image from the right eye because it is misaligned. A specialist will treat this by forcing the brain to use the weak eye, often by patching the strong eye.
Actionable Steps: Do not force long periods of eye covering tonight. Monitor strictly for head injury signs. Book the pediatric ophthalmology appointment first thing in the morning.

